NAME
       xorcat - undo (or make) a single-byte XOR over hex text (Cyber Range)

SYNOPSIS
       xorcat -k KEY [FILE]
       xorcat --brute [FILE]
       xorcat -e -k KEY [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
       XOR ("exclusive or") with one byte is the simplest way to scramble data:
       every byte is combined with the same key byte, and doing it again with
       the same key gives the original back. Malware and puzzles use it to hide
       text from a quick look.

       xorcat reads hex digits (pairs such as 4f 2a 1c, spaces and line breaks
       are ignored) from FILE or standard input, XORs every byte with KEY and
       prints the result as text (bytes that aren't printable show as a dot).

       With only 255 possible keys, a single-byte XOR is easy to break: --brute
       tries every key and prints only the results that are all plain text.

       xorcat is a Cyber Range practice tool; it works only inside the range.

OPTIONS
       -k KEY, --key=KEY
              The key byte, 0 to 255, in decimal (42) or hex (0x2a).

       -b, --brute
              Try every key from 1 to 255; show each one whose result is plain
              text.

       -e, --encode
              The other way round: read text and print it XORed with KEY, as
              hex.

EXAMPLES
           xorcat -k 0x2a secret.hex
           xorcat --brute secret.hex
           echo hello | xorcat -e -k 7

SEE ALSO
       xxd(1), od(1), base64(1)
